I had always assumed that sailing was something you either grew up with or never did at all. My parents were not sailors, and the closest I had come to a boat was a ferry crossing on a family holiday. So when a friend suggested that we sign up for a beginners' course on the south coast, I laughed. A week later, I found myself filling in the application form.
{1}
That first soaking turned out to be the most useful thing that could have happened. Once you have been in the water and climbed back out, the worst is over, and the fear that had been making me stiff and clumsy simply disappeared. By the end of the day I was actually enjoying myself.
{2}
All that study paid off on the fourth day, when the wind picked up and Jen sent us out in pairs without her. Suddenly the diagrams made sense. My partner, the retired teacher, called out the commands while I handled the ropes, and for the first time we were sailing rather than simply being carried along.
{3}
Looking back, it is that moment, rather than the calm afternoons, that I remember most clearly. Panic would have been the natural reaction, but we had been taught what to do, and we did it. There is a particular kind of confidence that comes from discovering that your training holds when it is tested.
{4}
Forty, it turns out, is not too late to start something new. It may even be the ideal age: old enough to know that falling in is not the end of the world, and young enough to climb back into the boat.

Few emotions have a worse reputation than boredom. We treat it as a problem to be solved, usually by reaching for a phone, and parents who once told restless children to go and find something to do now feel obliged to provide a constant stream of entertainment. Yet a growing body of research suggests that this restless state may serve a purpose.
{1}
Psychologists have proposed several explanations for this effect. The most persuasive is that boredom acts as a signal, telling us that our current activity is no longer rewarding and prompting the mind to search elsewhere. Seen in this light, it is less an emotion to be avoided than an alarm worth listening to.
{2}
None of this is to suggest that boredom is always benign. Chronic boredom, the kind that persists for months rather than minutes, has been linked to depression and risky behaviour, and in certain jobs it can be dangerous: a security guard or a pilot who loses concentration may cause real harm.
{3}
What follows from this for the rest of us is surprisingly modest. Nobody is advocating hours of enforced idleness. The suggestion is simply that we might occasionally resist the urge to fill every empty moment, and see where the mind goes when it is left to its own devices.
{4}
